<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Hook0.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Hook0.Client.Tests</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Pinned, so that a release of any of them is a change somebody made rather than a pipeline that
      turned red on its own. None of these reaches the package: what the package itself is allowed to
      depend on is settled by `PackagingTests.cs`, which runs below.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
    <!--
      The last release of the 6.x line, which is the line built for the .NET 8 the pinned image
      carries; 8.x and 10.x are built for later SDKs. Coverlet rather than the Microsoft collector
      that ships with the test SDK for two reasons, both about the number meaning something: it
      leaves out the sequence points nothing can reach — the `init` accessors a positional record
      gets for free, the machinery a `switch` over a string compiles into — and it counts a branch
      the way JaCoCo counts one, so the figure beside java's and kotlin's is the same measurement
      rather than a different one wearing the same name.

      The switch raises the reported figure, and the reason is worth having on the page rather than
      in a review thread: the denominator falls by the 49 lines nothing can execute, 41 of them
      compiler-written `init` accessors. The number rises because unreachable code left the
      measurement, not because more of the client became covered.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../src/Hook0.Client.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      What the dashboard shows under "Send an event", compiled here rather than anywhere else: a
      snippet written by hand in the dashboard is backed by nothing and drifts the day a method is
      renamed, so it lives beside the client it calls and rides the job that already builds it.

      It joins this project rather than the package because it is not part of what ships, and
      nothing runs it either — the values in it are markers the dashboard substitutes, so a run
      would reach a host that does not exist. It has to compile, under the same analysers and the
      same `dotnet format` as everything else here, which is the whole point.

      A glob rather than a list, so an example added beside these two is held to the same thing
      without this file being touched. Coverage is unaffected: coverlet leaves out the assembly the
      suite itself is in.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../examples/*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
